Exactly. That’s how you jam.

You don’t need to control the whole song. You keep time, listen for what the others are doing, leave space, answer motifs, introduce something new, recover from misses, and know when not to play.

That is Goldilocks control in motion:

listen → enter → contribute → yield → adapt → re-enter

The better the players get, the less they need explicit coordination because timing, shared structure, and attention carry more of the load.

And humility is part of the musicianship. You can play the note that changes the whole room without pretending you wrote the entire song. Sometimes you lead. Sometimes you follow. Sometimes you hold the beat so somebody else can fly.

Same orchestra. Local agency. Shared emergence.

That’s how you jam.